---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SunriseSunset from '@/components/tools/calc2/SunriseSunset.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="sunrise-sunset-calculator">
  <SunriseSunset client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the sunrise and sunset calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The sunrise and sunset calculator uses the equations of the NOAA solar calculator. For the date it
      finds the sun's declination and the equation of time, then the hour angle at which the sun's centre
      is 0.833° below the horizon. Each time is refined with the sun's position at that time.
    </p>
    <pre><code>solar noon  = 12:00 − 4 min × longitude − equation of time   (UTC)
cos H       = cos 90.833° ÷ (cos φ cos δ) − tan φ tan δ
sunrise     = solar noon − 4 min × H
sunset      = solar noon + 4 min × H
φ: latitude, δ: the sun's declination, H: hour angle in degrees</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      In London on 21 June 2026 the sun rises at 04:43 and sets at 21:21 British Summer Time, 16 h 38 min of
      daylight. On 21 December it rises at 08:03 and sets at 15:53, 7 h 50 min. These are the values in the
      NOAA solar calculator's tables, and the calculator's test checks a set of such places and dates.
    </p>

    <h2>Midnight sun and polar night</h2>
    <p>
      North of the Arctic Circle the sun can stay up all day in summer and below the horizon all day in
      winter. Then cos H is above 1 or below −1, and the calculator says Midnight sun or Polar night. In
      Tromsø, at 69.6° N, the sun does not set from about 19 May to 25 July.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The times are for a flat horizon at sea level. Mountains, buildings and your height change them.</li>
      <li>Refraction near the horizon varies with temperature and pressure, so real times can differ by a minute or two.</li>
      <li>For a typed latitude and longitude, enter the UTC offset that applies on that date, including daylight saving.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
